                <h1 class="content-title">Change & <span class="gradient-text">Release Management</span></h1>
                <p class="content-subtitle">
                    Master modern IT change enablement in ServiceNow — Normal, Standard, and Emergency change models, 
                    automated risk calculation, CI conflict detection, CAB Workbench governance, and change task execution.
                </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Section 1: Governance Mission -->
            <section class="content-section" id="change-mission">
                <h2>Governance Mission & ITIL Alignment</h2>
                <p>
                    According to ITIL v4, the purpose of <strong>Change Enablement</strong> is to 
                    <em>"maximize the number of successful service and product changes by ensuring that risks have been properly assessed, 
                    authorizing changes to proceed, and managing the change schedule."</em>
                </p>
                <p>
                    In an enterprise environment, over <strong>70% of unplanned outages</strong> are historically caused by unauthorized, 
                    untested, or conflicting changes to infrastructure and code. ServiceNow Change Management strikes the balance between 
                    <strong>speed of innovation (DevOps/CI-CD)</strong> and <strong>operational stability</strong>.
                </p>

                <div class="info-card info-card--important">
                    <div class="info-card-title">Key Architectural Principle</div>
                    <p>
                        Change Management does not perform the technical work directly; it orchestrates the <strong>risk governance</strong>, 
                        <strong>schedules maintenance windows</strong>, <strong>secures multi-stakeholder approvals</strong>, and decomposes 
                        execution into concrete <strong>Change Tasks (<code>change_task</code>)</strong>.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 2: 3 Core Change Types -->
            <section class="content-section" id="change-models">
                <h2>The 3 Core Change Types & Modern Change Models</h2>
                <p>
                    ServiceNow classifies all modifications into three primary operational categories, each following distinct 
                    state transitions, approval gates, and lead times:
                </p>

                <div class="feature-grid">
                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>Standard Change</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            <strong>Pre-authorized, low-risk, recurring, and procedural.</strong> Templated via the Standard Change Catalog 
                            (<code>std_change_producer_version</code>). Bypasses CAB review entirely and moves directly to the Scheduled state upon creation.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>Normal Change</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            <strong>Non-emergency changes requiring full governance.</strong> Follows the complete lifecycle: Technical Assessment, 
                            Risk Scoring, Conflict Detection, Peer Review, CAB Approval, Implementation during Maintenance Window, and PIR.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>Emergency Change</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            <strong>Urgent fixes for critical outages (P1/P2) or zero-day security vulnerabilities.</strong> 
                            Bypasses standard lead times. Fast-tracked through the Emergency CAB (ECAB) with verbal/expedited approval and retrospective documentation.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-card info-card--tip">
                    <div class="info-card-title">NextGen Change Models (chg_model)</div>
                    <p>
                        Starting in the Quebec/Rome releases, ServiceNow introduced <strong>Change Models (<code>chg_model</code>)</strong>, 
                        allowing administrators to define custom state transition flows, conditions, and UI experiences per model 
                        (e.g., Automated Cloud Pipeline Model, Database Patching Model, Site Reliability Engineering Model) 
                        without modifying global business rules.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 3: Schema & Data Structure -->
            <section class="content-section" id="table-schema">
                <h2>Schema Architecture & Table Registry</h2>
                <p>
                    The Change Management database hierarchy centers on <code>change_request</code>, extending <code>task</code> 
                    and orchestrating child execution tasks, schedule definitions, and approvals:
                </p>

                <table class="data-table">
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Table Name</th>
                            <th>Parent Extension</th>
                            <th>Operational Scope</th>
                            <th>Key Columns & Data Types</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>change_request</code></td>
                            <td><code>task</code></td>
                            <td>Master Request for Change (RFC) envelope.</td>
                            <td>
                                <code>type</code> (Choice), <code>chg_model</code> (Ref), <code>risk</code> (Integer), <code>scope</code> (Choice), 
                                <code>start_date</code> (DateTime), <code>end_date</code> (DateTime), <code>cab_required</code> (Boolean), 
                                <code>cab_date</code> (Date), <code>review_status</code> (Choice), <code>conflict_status</code> (Choice)
                            </td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>change_task</code></td>
                            <td><code>task</code></td>
                            <td>Discrete technical implementation steps.</td>
                            <td>
                                <code>change_request</code> (Ref), <code>change_task_type</code> (Choice: Planning, Implementation, Testing, Review), 
                                <code>state</code> (Choice), <code>planned_start_date</code> (DateTime)
                            </td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>std_change_producer_version</code></td>
                            <td>None (Base Table)</td>
                            <td>Approved Standard Change template catalog.</td>
                            <td>
                                <code>std_change_proposal</code> (Ref), <code>version</code> (Integer), <code>active</code> (Boolean)
                            </td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>cmn_schedule_blackout</code></td>
                            <td><code>cmn_schedule</code></td>
                            <td>Blackout calendar windows where changes are strictly forbidden.</td>
                            <td>
                                <code>name</code>, <code>type</code>, <code>applies_to</code> (Ref to CMDB CI or Class)
                            </td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>cmn_schedule_maintenance</code></td>
                            <td><code>cmn_schedule</code></td>
                            <td>Authorized operational windows for production changes.</td>
                            <td>
                                <code>name</code>, <code>type</code>, <code>applies_to</code>
                            </td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>sysapproval_approver</code></td>
                            <td>None (Base Table)</td>
                            <td>Individual sign-offs generated for CAB and Technical leads.</td>
                            <td>
                                <code>sysapproval</code> (Ref to <code>task</code>), <code>approver</code> (Ref to <code>sys_user</code>), <code>state</code> (Choice)
                            </td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 4: State Model -->
            <section class="content-section" id="state-lifecycle">
                <h2>Normal & Emergency State Model Lifecycle</h2>
                <p>
                    Normal and Emergency changes transition through sequential state gates. Moving to the next state requires 
                    fulfilling strict validation conditions:
                </p>

                <div class="diagram-block">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Normal Change Request State Transition Lifecycle</div>
                    <div class="mermaid">
                    graph TD
                        NEW["New (-5)<br>Change logged & technical scope defined"] --> ASSESS["Assess (-4)<br>Peer review, technical viability & risk scoring"]
                        ASSESS --> AUTH["Authorize (-3)<br>CAB review & multi-level stakeholder sign-offs"]
                        
                        AUTH -->|CAB Approved| SCHED["Scheduled (-2)<br>Assigned to maintenance window without CI conflicts"]
                        AUTH -->|CAB Rejected| NEW
                        
                        SCHED --> IMPL["Implement (-1)<br>Change Tasks (CTASKs) executed in production"]
                        IMPL --> REV["Review (0)<br>Post-Implementation Review (PIR) validation"]
                        
                        REV --> CLOSED["Closed (3)<br>Successfully deployed & validated"]
                        
                        NEW -->|Canceled by Requester| CANC["Canceled (4)"]
                        ASSESS -->|Canceled| CANC
                        AUTH -->|Canceled| CANC
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="tabs-container">
                    <div class="tabs-header">
                        <button class="tab-btn active" data-tab="tab-normal-states">Normal Change States</button>
                        <button class="tab-btn" data-tab="tab-emergency-states">Emergency Change States</button>
                        <button class="tab-btn" data-tab="tab-closure-codes">Closure Codes</button>
                    </div>

                    <div class="tab-panel active" id="tab-normal-states">
                        <h3>Normal Change Request States (Integers)</h3>
                        <table class="data-table">
                            <thead>
                                <tr>
                                    <th>State Label</th>
                                    <th>Integer Value</th>
                                    <th>Mandatory Criteria & State Gate Rules</th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>New</strong></td>
                                    <td><code>-5</code></td>
                                    <td>Initial drafting. Description, justification, risk questions, and planned CI must be populated.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Assess</strong></td>
                                    <td><code>-4</code></td>
                                    <td>Technical peer review. Assignment group manager verifies rollback plan and implementation plan.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Authorize</strong></td>
                                    <td><code>-3</code></td>
                                    <td>Formal CAB evaluation. Approval records (<code>sysapproval_approver</code>) generated for CAB members.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Scheduled</strong></td>
                                    <td><code>-2</code></td>
                                    <td>Approved. Awaiting arrival of planned start date (`start_date`) within maintenance window.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Implement</strong></td>
                                    <td><code>-1</code></td>
                                    <td>Active deployment in progress. Resolver engineers execute child <code>change_task</code> records.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Review</strong></td>
                                    <td><code>0</code></td>
                                    <td>Deployment complete. PIR conducted to evaluate success, downtime variance, and incidents generated.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Closed</strong></td>
                                    <td><code>3</code></td>
                                    <td>Final read-only state.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Canceled</strong></td>
                                    <td><code>4</code></td>
                                    <td>Change aborted before deployment.</td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>

                    <div class="tab-panel" id="tab-emergency-states">
                        <h3>Emergency Change Workflow</h3>
                        <p>
                            Emergency changes bypass the standard <em>Assess</em> gate and move straight to <strong>Authorize</strong> 
                            where expedited approval from the <strong>Emergency CAB (ECAB)</strong> is secured:
                        </p>
                        <ul class="feature-bullet-list">
                            <li><strong>Fast-Track Authorization:</strong> Single ECAB approval triggers the move to <em>Scheduled</em> or immediately to <em>Implement</em>.</li>
                            <li><strong>Retroactive Change Logging:</strong> In extreme P1 outages, engineers may restore the service first and log the Emergency Change within 24 hours for audit compliance.</li>
                            <li><strong>Mandatory PIR:</strong> All Emergency changes require mandatory Post-Implementation Review to analyze why the emergency arose.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>

                    <div class="tab-panel" id="tab-closure-codes">
                        <h3>Closure Codes (<code>close_code</code>)</h3>
                        <table class="data-table">
                            <thead>
                                <tr>
                                    <th>Closure Code</th>
                                    <th>Operational Meaning</th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><code>Successful</code></td>
                                    <td>Deployed without issues or service disruptions.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><code>Successful with Issues</code></td>
                                    <td>Deployed successfully, but minor unexpected defects or incidents were logged.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><code>Unsuccessful</code></td>
                                    <td>Deployment failed; rollback plan executed to restore previous baseline.</td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 5: Risk Assessment & Conflict Engine -->
            <section class="content-section" id="risk-and-conflict">
                <h2>Automated Risk Calculation & Conflict Detection Engine</h2>
                <p>
                    Before any change reaches the CAB, ServiceNow executes two automated analytical engines to protect 
                    infrastructure availability:
                </p>

                <div class="feature-grid">
                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>Risk Calculation Engine</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            Calculates the <strong>Risk (<code>risk</code>)</strong> value (Very High, High, Moderate, Low) dynamically using:
                            <br>1. <strong>Risk Conditions:</strong> Evaluates criteria such as Class of CI (Production vs Test), Number of Impacted Users, and Complexity.
                            <br>2. <strong>Risk Assessment Survey:</strong> Structured questionnaires scored via the Assessment engine (<code>asmt_assessment_instance</code>).
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>Conflict Detection Engine</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            Analyzes planned <code>start_date</code> and <code>end_date</code> against:
                            <br>1. <strong>Blackout Schedules:</strong> Identifies changes scheduled during frozen fiscal/holiday periods.
                            <br>2. <strong>Maintenance Windows:</strong> Flags changes scheduled outside approved operational windows.
                            <br>3. <strong>CI Dependency Collisions:</strong> Warns when two concurrent changes touch the same CI or parent/child CIs in the CMDB.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 6: CAB Workbench -->
            <section class="content-section" id="cab-workbench">
                <h2>CAB Workbench (<code>cab_workbench</code>) & Approval Governance</h2>
                <p>
                    The <strong>Change Advisory Board (CAB) Workbench</strong> provides a dedicated cockpit for CAB managers 
                    to plan, chair, and execute formal change review meetings:
                </p>

                <div class="feature-grid">
                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>Automated Agenda Generation</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            Automatically aggregates all Normal changes in the <em>Authorize</em> state where <code>cab_required = true</code> 
                            and <code>cab_date</code> matches the meeting schedule, assigning per-ticket presentation time slots.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>Visual Calendar & Conflict Radar</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            Visualizes all planned outages on an interactive timeline alongside CI service health maps, allowing 
                            stakeholders to verify that changes do not conflict with business-critical operations.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>Live In-Meeting Approval Voting</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            CAB managers can record approvals, rejections, or requests for additional data directly in the workbench UI, 
                            instantly updating <code>sysapproval_approver</code> records and transitioning tickets to <em>Scheduled</em>.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

